 \Psi=2a(d-m)\bigl(g-F(m)\bigr)
       -\frac{a^2}{2}(d-m)^2-3V.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Set $$p=\frac{1-m}{2},\qquad
w=\min\left\{2p,\;4\sqrt{\frac32}\,p^{3/2}\right\}.$$ Then $$\begin{aligned}
W_1(f)&\le w,\label{eq:first-level-bound}\\
\operatorname{Var}(d)&\le r[(1-r)w+rh^2].\label{eq:variance-bound}
\end{aligned}$$ If $d_{\rm o}(x)=(d(x)-d(-x))/2$ is the odd part of $d$, then $$\label{eq:odd-variance-bound}
\mathbb Ed_{\rm o}^2\le r[(1-r^2)w+r^2h^2].$$

For every coordinate edge, $$\label{cen:edge-bound}
 D_i d\,D_i\!\left(\frac d{H(d)}\right)
 \ge (D_i g)^2+\Phi\bigl((D_i g)^2\bigr).$$ The functions $j$ and $\Phi$ are nonnegative, increasing, and convex. Furthermore, $\Phi'(v)/v$ is increasing for $v>0$.

At a crossing in the central band, $$\label{cen:four-constants}
 \frac{\Phi(M)}M<.0063,\qquad p_*<.019,\qquad
 \frac{p_*}M<.0532,\qquad \frac\Delta M<.008.$$

Under the central-band crossing hypotheses of Proposition <a href="#prop:central" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:central">10</a>, for every $0\le u\le1$, $$\label{cen:pointwise-bound}
 D(u)-2\alpha m|R|+.88m^2
 \ge C_0(g^2-M)^2+.20(u^2-y^2)^2.$$ The inequality is strict whenever $u\ne y$.

For every $-1<d<1$, with $V,\Psi$ defined by <a href="#mid:V" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="mid:V">[mid:V]</a> and <a href="#mid:Psi" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="mid:Psi">[mid:Psi]</a>, one has $$\label{mid:pointwise-bounds}
 \begin{array}{ll}
 \Psi\ge(\frac12-2m^2)V^2,&0\le m\le\frac15,\\[2pt]
 \Psi\ge-\frac1{100}V,&\frac15\le m\le\frac23,\\[2pt]
 \Psi\ge-\frac1{50}V,&\frac23\le m\le\frac{37}{50}.
 \end{array}$$

Let $u_0\ge0$ be a real function on the cube, $\tau\ge0$, and $r=e^{-2\tau}$. For every $q>0$, $q\ne1$, put $p_q=1+r(q-1)$. Then $$\label{lb:norm-moments}
 \mathbb E(T_{e^{-\tau}}u_0)^q
 \begin{cases}
 \displaystyle\le(\mathbb Eu_0^{p_q})^{q/p_q},&q>1,\\[2pt]
 \displaystyle\ge(\mathbb Eu_0^{p_q})^{q/p_q},&0<q<1.
 \end{cases}$$
